#X text 12 620 THE CANVAS IS 448x207 \, exactly half of the iPhone 11's 896x414 landscape points \, so PdParty scales everything 2x with no letterboxing and the font sizes above are half their on-screen size. EVERY GUI OBJECT HAS BOTH A SEND AND A RECEIVE NAME -- with empty or a dash they parse \, instantiate \, participate \, and are INVISIBLE., f 80;
#X text 400 620 THE DETECTOR LIVES HERE AND IT HAS TO. UDP is fire and forget \, so the Organelle cannot know the phone is gone -- only the end that stops hearing can. Every datagram restarts the 1500 ms timer and sets ok \; if it ever fires we say NO-LINK. The default is NO-LINK rather than ok \, so a scene opened before the Organelle is running does not look connected when it is not., f 60;
#X text 12 780 NO print ANYWHERE IN HERE. PdParty transmits print as /pdparty/print OSC \, so one print on a 2 Hz stream is a flood -- 138 packets in the time it takes to drag a fader once., f 80;
#X text 200 620 hb NEEDS NO BRANCH: it falls out of route's reject having already restarted the timer above \, which is the only thing it is for., f 40;
#X text 12 880 THE 22-UNIT MARGIN ON THE RIGHT IS THE NOTCH. On an iPhone 11 the speaker and camera dip into the screen \, and in landscape that eats about 44 points off ONE edge -- 22 canvas units \, since everything here renders at 2x. PdParty does NOT apply the safe-area inset for you \, so the patch has to. ONLY THE RIGHT IS INSET: turning the phone puts the notch on whichever side you want \, so the fix is to pick a side and hold it that way rather than to lose 44 points at both ends. The bottom keeps 17 units clear for the home indicator., f 80;
#X obj 960 222 route re-wire clear-alert test-note drums;
#X obj 960 282 s \$0-rw-ack;
#X obj 1150 282 s \$0-ca-ack;
#X obj 1330 282 route m_volca m_404;
#X obj 1330 342 s \$0-tv-ack;
#X obj 1530 342 s \$0-t4-ack;
#X obj 12 1300 r \$0-rw-press;
#X msg 12 1350 re-wire;
#X obj 200 1300 r \$0-ca-press;
#X msg 200 1350 clear-alert;
#X obj 420 1300 r \$0-tv-press;
#X msg 420 1350 test-note m_volca;
#X obj 680 1300 r \$0-t4-press;
#X msg 680 1350 test-note m_404;
#X obj 12 1420 list append;
#X obj 12 1480 oscformat cutit;
#X obj 12 1540 s #osc-out;
#X text 12 1620 THE OUTBOUND HALF \, AND IT IS THE ONLY THING ON THIS PHONE THAT CAN CHANGE THE INSTRUMENT. Four buttons \, one shared oscformat \, and the command travels as ARGUMENTS of /cutit rather than as address components -- oscparse on the other end flattens both to the same list \, so this is simply the cheaper of the two to build. ⛔ list append BEFORE oscformat: a message box holding one word sends that word as a SELECTOR and oscformat wants a list. THE SPELLINGS HERE ARE LOAD-BEARING AND ARE CHECKED BY test/gate/phone-assert.sh -- they must equal u_net's own route arguments exactly \, because this scene is the only sender there is and a typo would be invisible until somebody was standing at the rig., f 80;
#X text 12 1920 ⛔ THE BUTTON AND ITS LAMP ARE TWO OBJECTS AND THAT IS NOT TIDINESS -- IT IS A LOOP GUARD. Measured in 0.49: a bng whose send and receive names DIFFER re-sends when it RECEIVES \, out of both its outlet and its send name. So feeding the acknowledgment back into the button that sent the command would fire the command again \, which would fire another acknowledgment \, for ever \, at network speed. The button sends on -press and receives on -nc \, which NOTHING ever writes \; the lamp receives the ack on -ack and sends to -lamp \, which nothing reads. Both carry both names because PdParty renders no iemgui that is missing either., f 80;
#X text 960 420 THE LAMP FLASHES ON THE ORGANELLE'S ANSWER \, NOT ON YOUR FINGER \, which is the whole point of it. A button that lights when pressed tells you the one thing never in doubt. clear-alert and the two test notes prove themselves anyway -- the row clears \, the note sounds -- and re-wire has nothing else whatever to show for itself. ⚠️ route STRAIGHT INTO route with no list append between them: a matched remainder whose first atom is a symbol arrives as a message with that symbol as its SELECTOR \, which is what the second route matches on., f 60;
